<section class="aim" id="aim">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">01 — the single aim</div>
    <div class="aim-statement">We exist to gather together everyone who wants Scotland to leave the union. Nothing else.</div>
    <p class="aim-note">No currency policy. No tax policy. No rulebook. No one in charge. You can disagree with every other new radical about everything except one thing, and still belong. It's this: Scotland doesn't need permission to leave the union.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sovereignty" id="sovereignty">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">02 — leaving the UK</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Why is it that we need to ask?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sov-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Two things here are barely in dispute, even among committed unionists: that the Scottish people are sovereign, and that sovereignty means Scotland can leave the union whenever a majority of Scots wish it — without needing anyone's permission. A convinced unionist can accept both of those and still be a committed unionist.</p>
        <p>What makes someone a new radical isn't believing either of those things. It's wanting Scotland to leave, on the strength of a sovereignty nobody seriously disputes.</p>
        <p>That sovereignty isn't an abstract principle — it's rooted in Scotland's own constitutional tradition, distinct from Westminster's. The Claim of Right, passed in 1689 not by a Parliament but by a Convention of Estates, affirmed that power in Scotland flows from the people, conditionally granted to their rulers — the reverse of England's doctrine of untouchable parliamentary sovereignty.</p>
        <div class="aim-statement" style="font-size:clamp(18px,2.6vw,24px); border-left-width:4px; padding-left:16px; margin-top:24px; max-width:none;">Scottish new radicals are the sovereign people of Scotland who will exercise that sovereignty to take Scotland out of the union — without asking.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="member-card">
        <div class="card-top">
          <svg class="card-cap" viewBox="0 0 40 46" fill="none"><path d="M20 2C9 8 4 18 6 30c1 6 6 10 14 10s13-4 14-10c2-12-3-22-14-28z" fill="#0067bf"/><rect x="3" y="40" width="34" height="4" rx="1" fill="#0067bf"/></svg>
          <div class="card-no">member<strong>0001</strong></div>
        </div>
        <p class="card-quote">"I am a new radical. I assert the Scottish people's inalienable right to complete freedom, the exercise of our sovereignty and the integrity of our national territory."</p>
        <p class="card-quote2">By the authority of our constitutional tradition enshrined in the Declaration of Arbroath 1320 and the active authority of the Claim of Right 1689, our sovereignty is permanent. Therefore we need not ask for our freedom; we simply claim it, and declare that Scotland's connection to the union is, and of right ought to be, totally dissolved.</p>
        <div class="card-foot">the scottish new radicals · membership card</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="history" id="history">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">03 — the historic hook</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">The same struggle, two centuries apart</h2>
      <p class="lede">The name isn't a marketing choice. It's a claim of lineage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hist-grid">
      <div class="hist-col">
        <div class="hist-year">1820</div>
        <h3>the radicals</h3>
        <p>Weavers, artisans and tradesmen across the west of Scotland struck for democratic rights and self-government, in a movement that ended in trial and execution but became a founding memory of Scottish working-class politics.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hist-col">
        <div class="hist-year">today</div>
        <h3>new radicals</h3>
        <p>No party machine, no funded office, no professional class of organisers — the same low-cost, high-conviction shape as 1820, rebuilt for a network that can be run from a shared inbox and a printer's tab.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="how" id="join-explainer">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">04 — how joining works</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Three steps, no admin team</h2>
      <p class="lede">The whole system runs without a bank account, because nothing about it requires one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">step one</div>
        <h3>sign up online</h3>
        <p>Add your name to the list below. That's the only thing that happens on the website — no payment, no account, no data beyond what's needed to send you a card.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">step two</div>
        <h3>send an SAE</h3>
        <p>Post a stamped-addressed envelope to the holding address you're sent. You cover the postage — it's the only cost of belonging, and it's what keeps the network free to run.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">step three</div>
        <h3>get your number</h3>
        <p>A sequentially numbered card comes back in your own envelope — Member 0001 through Member x000 — printed on credit paid for by donors, drawn down one batch at a time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="thinking" id="thinking">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">05 — new thinking on the union</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">The argument, in short</h2>
      <p class="lede">Supporting reading for anyone deciding whether to join — or arguing with someone who won't.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="think-grid">
      <div class="think-card">
        <h3>What the Claim of Right actually says</h3>
        <p>A short guide to the 1689 Claim of Right — passed by a Convention of Estates, not a Parliament, and entrenched "unalterable... in all time coming" by the 1707 Act for Securing the Protestant Religion — and why it makes the "permission" framing of independence a category error.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="think-card">
        <h3>Why a movement with no bank account is harder to stop</h3>
        <p>On the design logic behind zero-overhead organising: no treasurer to pressure, no accounts to freeze, no office to raid, no leadership to buy off.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="think-card">
        <h3>Democratic self-determination, not grievance</h3>
        <p>The case for framing independence as an ordinary democratic right rather than a complaint — and why that framing tends to bring in people other movements don't reach.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="think-card">
        <h3>1820 to now: what actually carried over</h3>
        <p>What the Radicals of 1820 can and can't teach a 21st-century movement — and where the comparison is a source of discipline rather than nostalgia.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:14px; color:var(--stone); margin-top:24px;">Want the deeper historical case for why the 1707 union itself was never validly consented to? Read the full argument at <a href="https://endtheunion.scot" style="color:var(--azure-deep);">endtheunion.scot</a>.</p>
    <div class="think-more">
      <p>New essays go out through the newsletter first, then into a public archive anyone can read and share.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#join">get the newsletter</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

